  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "If $|u\\cdot v|+\u2016u+v\u2016=34$ and $\u2016v\u2016=6$, then what is the smallest possible value of $\u2016u\u2016?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =|u\\cdot v|+\u2016u+v\u2016 \\\\  & \\le \u2016u\u2016\\cdot \u2016v\u2016+\u2016u+v\u2016 \\\\  & =\u2016u\u2016\\cdot (6)+\u2016u+v\u2016 \\\\  & =6\u2016u\u2016+\u2016u+v\u2016.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\u2016u\u2016+\u2016u+v\u2016 & \\le 6\u2016u\u2016+(\u2016u\u2016+\u2016v\u2016) \\\\  & =6\u2016u\u2016+(\u2016u\u2016+6) \\\\  & =7\u2016u\u2016+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & \\le 7\u2016u\u2016+6 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7\u2016u\u2016 \\\\ 4 & \\le \u2016u\u2016.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $\u2016u\u2016$ is $4.$",
